            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.069,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "93"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What properties are common to all circles, big and small? You have studied one such property in Grade 7. Humans must have noticed it after observing many circular patterns in nature. Every circle has a centre. All points on the circle are at equal distance from the centre. We turn this observation around and make it the definition of a circle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.213,
            0.824,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " List some objects from nature that resemble a circle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.251,
            0.399,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.289,
            0.856,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Jamuna has a circular piece of paper. She is trying to locate its centre. Amina gives her a suggestion. She follows the instructions and is thrilled to find that it works. Can you guess what Amina told her?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.374,
            0.334,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.1 Definitions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.407,
            0.887,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When we talk of mathematical shapes such as circles, triangles and squares, we always assume that the figures are drawn on a piece of paper — a two-dimensional plane."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.472,
            0.48,
            0.7
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.147,
                0.472,
                0.48,
                0.7
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_1_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.153,
                0.706,
                0.455,
                0.724
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.3: Circle, Centre A, Chord BC"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.475,
            0.878,
            0.746
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "circle",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the set of all points on the plane that are equidistant from a given point on that plane. The set of points that satisfy a given condition is also called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "locus",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of points that satisfy the condition. Using this term, a circle can also be described as the locus of points that are equidistant from a given point. The given point is the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "centre",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the circle. The distance from the centre to any point on the circle is the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "radius",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the circle."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.75,
            0.886,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Fig 5.3, A is the centre of the circle. All points on the plane at a distance equal to the length of AB from A form a circle with centre A and radius equal to the length of AB."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.807,
            0.886,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let B and C be two points on the circle. The line segment BC is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "chord",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the circle. The angle subtended by the chord BC at the centre is angle BAC. A chord passing through the centre of a circle is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "diameter",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
